/* =============================================================================
   ODISHA — data for state-tool-engine.js. See assets/js/data/kerala.js for the
   schema reference and assets/js/data/jharkhand.js/bihar.js for the fullest
   prior examples of this file's research/honesty discipline (primary-source
   extraction wherever possible, rule-by-rule citation, honest hedging over
   invented figures, explicit flagging of conflicting sources). Odisha is the
   first "East zone" state added to this app after Bihar and Jharkhand.

   PRIMARY SOURCE #1 — "ORISSA LEAVE RULES, 1966": this session fetched and
   read (via pdftotext on the actual PDF, hosted at dhe.odisha.gov.in — Odisha's
   own Directorate of Higher Education, re-hosting the gazetted rules text) the
   FULL RULE TEXT of the Orissa Leave Rules, 1966 (Notification No. 27194/F,
   dated 9.8.1966), Odisha's own leave-account instrument (Chapter VI of the
   Orissa Service Code governs a narrow set of excepted categories instead —
   see Rule 2(2) of these Rules — but the 1966 Rules are what apply to the
   ordinary State Civil Service employee this app models). DIRECTLY READ,
   RULE BY RULE: Earned Leave (Rule 7) — one-eleventh of the period spent on
   duty for a permanent Government servant, ceasing to accrue once the EL due
   reaches 180 days, with a maximum single grant of 120 days (180 days if any
   part is spent outside India/Nepal, for Class I State Civil Service, subject
   to the India-spent portion not exceeding 120 days in the aggregate). Half
   Pay Leave (Rule 9(1)) — 20 days per completed year of service, grantable on
   medical certificate or for private affairs. Commuted Leave (Rule 9(3)) —
   commutation of HPL not exceeding half the HPL due, on medical certificate
   only, capped at 240 days in the entire service, with twice the commuted
   amount debited against the HPL account. Leave Not Due (Rule 10) — up to 360
   days in the entire service (up to 90 days at a time and 180 days in all on
   grounds other than medical certificate), debited against HPL the officer
   earns on return to duty. Extraordinary Leave (Rule 13) — for a permanent
   Government servant or one with 3+ years' continuous service, no fixed
   ceiling stated in this Rule itself; for others, capped at 2 months (4 months
   with a medical certificate; 18 months for tuberculosis/leprosy treatment
   under specific conditions per Notification No. 5959-Codes-70/F, dated
   17.2.1971) — Rule 17(6) confirms NO leave salary is admissible during
   Extraordinary Leave. THIS SESSION EXPLICITLY FLAGS THAT THE FETCHED TEXT IS
   THE 1966 RULES AS ORIGINALLY NOTIFIED (with inline amendments only through
   1973) — it does NOT itself reflect whether Odisha has since raised the EL
   accumulation ceiling to 300 days (the near-universal post-6th-CPC figure
   most peer states in this app now use) or removed the Rule 17(3) leave-salary
   rupee caps (Rs. 750/Rs. 250 — clearly stale 1966-era figures against any
   current pay scale). This session's searches did not locate a specific
   amendment notification confirming a 300-day EL ceiling for Odisha —
   the 180-day accrual-cessation and 120/180-day single-grant figures from the
   primary text are used below AS READ, with an explicit hedge that a more
   recent ceiling may apply; confirm your own current leave-account rules with
   your DDO/HRMS before relying on the 180-day figure specifically.

   CASUAL LEAVE & ADDITIONAL CASUAL LEAVE FOR WOMEN — PRIMARY-SOURCE-CONFIRMED,
   WITH A CORRECTED SECONDARY FIGURE: this session fetched Finance Department
   Office Memorandum No. 694A/F (FIN-CS2-ALW-0006-2024) via pdftotext — its own
   text states verbatim: "the benefit of 10 (ten) days of Additional Casual
   Leave in a calendar year in favour of women Government employees over and
   above the existing 10 days of Casual Leave and 5 days of Special Casual
   Leave." This CONFIRMS, IN THE PRIMARY TEXT ITSELF, base Casual Leave (10
   days) and Special Casual Leave (5 days), AND CORRECTS a secondary
   compilation (a consumer-facing leave-policy summary) read earlier this
   session, which claimed 12 additional days for women (total 27) — the
   primary O.M. explicitly says 10 additional days (total 25 for women: 10 CL
   + 5 SCL + 10 Additional CL). The 12/27 figure is NOT used below; this file
   uses the primary-confirmed 10/25 figure, with the conflict flagged here.
   The O.M.'s exact date-of-issue did not extract cleanly (the PDF is a
   scanned notification whose header stamp did not OCR), but its file path
   indicates a 2024 issue, and its own "FIN-CS2-ALW-0006-2024" file number
   confirms the year.

   MENSTRUAL LEAVE — A GENUINELY ODISHA-SPECIFIC PROVISION, NOT MODELLED IN ANY
   PEER STATE'S FILE IN THIS APP: confirmed by FIVE independent secondary news
   sources this session (Deccan Chronicle, The Statesman, TeamLease RegTech — a
   compliance-tracking firm, Women Entrepreneurs Review, and Madhyamam), all in
   agreement: announced by Deputy CM Pravati Parida on 15 August 2024 (78th
   Independence Day), with the policy notified 16 August 2024 — one day of paid
   menstrual leave per cycle, optional, to be availed "on the first or second
   day of the menstrual cycle," for women employees of both the State
   Government and the private sector. NONE of the five sources gave a specific
   notification/order number, so none is asserted here — confirm the exact
   order and any procedural requirements (intimation, certificate) with your
   DDO/HRMS before applying.

   MATERNITY / PATERNITY / CHILD CARE LEAVE — CONFIRMED VIA A SEPTEMBER 2024
   SURROGACY-SPECIFIC AMENDMENT (Deccan Herald/Careers360, in agreement): the
   Odisha Government extended Maternity Leave (180 days) and Paternity Leave
   (15 days, within 6 months of delivery) to "commissioning"/surrogate parents
   with fewer than two surviving children — reported specifically AS AN
   EXTENSION of the general 180-day/15-day entitlements, which this file reads
   as confirming those baseline figures for Odisha specifically (matching the
   familiar nationwide CCS-derived pattern several peer states in this app
   already use), though this session did not separately fetch the Odisha
   Service Code/Leave Rules' own maternity/paternity rule text or number.
   Child Care Leave (730 days, 2 eldest surviving children under 18, max 3
   spells/year, minimum 15 days per spell) is the same nationwide CCS-derived
   figure used by Jharkhand/Bihar/Uttarakhand/Rajasthan's files in this app —
   NOT independently confirmed as an Odisha-specific rule-numbered figure this
   session, offered only as the common comparison point. Confirm all three
   entitlements and any Odisha-specific rule number with your DDO/HRMS.

   PENSION SCHEME STATUS — NPS, WITH ODISHA HAVING ADOPTED THE UNIFIED PENSION
   SCHEME (UPS) AS AN OPTION, NOT OPS RESTORATION: this session's searches
   found NO evidence Odisha is among the OPS-restored states (Rajasthan,
   Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Punjab, Himachal Pradesh, per a general news
   round-up read this session) — Odisha remains on NPS by default for
   employees appointed on/after 1 January 2004. DIRECTLY READ from Odisha's own
   Finance Department NPS notification-index page (finance.odisha.gov.in) this
   session: the State Government's own NPS contribution was enhanced from 10%
   to 14% with effect from 1.4.2019, and — distinctively among most peer states
   in this app's roster — that same page lists a 21 March 2025 "Unified Pension
   Scheme" notification and an 11 April 2025 entry on "exercise of option for
   coverage under Unified Pension Scheme." Independent secondary corroboration
   (a UNI India wire report) states Odisha implemented UPS effective 1 April
   2025, as an option for NPS-covered employees, describing the scheme's own
   nationally-published terms (assured pension of 50% of average basic pay
   over the last 12 months before retirement for 25+ years' qualifying
   service, pro-rata for 10-25 years, family pension at 60% of the employee's
   own pension, minimum monthly pension Rs. 10,000 after 10+ years' service) —
   this session did NOT independently fetch Odisha's own UPS notification text
   to confirm these figures apply identically to Odisha's own scheme rather
   than only the Government of India's own parallel central-employee UPS.
   Confirm your own current scheme (NPS or UPS), PRAN, and any option deadline
   with your DDO/HRMS/the Odisha Finance Department before relying on this.

   GENERAL PROVIDENT FUND — "THE GENERAL PROVIDENT FUND (ORISSA) RULES, 1938,"
   WITH A DIRECTLY-FLAGGED RATE CONFLICT: this session found the 1938 Rules'
   own text (as reproduced in a legal bare-acts compilation) states a minimum
   subscription of 8% of emoluments (maximum: total emoluments) — but the
   Principal Accountant General (A&E), Odisha's OWN current GPF guideline page
   (cag.gov.in/ae/odisha), fetched this session, states "not less than ten
   percent of his emoluments and not more than his total emoluments." BOTH
   figures are presented here with this conflict flagged explicitly, rather
   than silently picking one — the AG's own page, being a live administrative
   page rather than the frozen 1938 text, is more likely to reflect the
   current minimum, but this session did not independently trace the specific
   amendment that raised it from 8% to 10%. The AG's page also confirms
   interest is credited annually "at the rate prescribed by Govt. of Odisha"
   (no current rate is asserted, since it is reset periodically) and that on
   quitting/retirement the credited balance becomes payable, and to the
   family/nominee on death. No specific advance/withdrawal rule text was
   located this session — use the GPF query type to request written
   confirmation of your own current terms rather than relying on an asserted
   rate.

   GROUP INSURANCE SCHEME (GIS) — STRONG PRIMARY CONFIRMATION OF STRUCTURE AND
   DATES, SECONDARY-ONLY ON CURRENT PREMIUM/COVER FIGURES: this session fetched
   and read (via pdftotext) Finance Department Resolution No. 3/F (2022,
   FIN-GIS2-MISC-0007-2022), "Comprehensive Guideline for implementation of
   Group Insurance Scheme in the State" — DIRECTLY CONFIRMED: the scheme began
   as an optional benefit from 1.3.1974 (Resolution No. 2594/F, dt. 25.1.1974),
   made compulsory from 1.4.1976 (Resolution No. 19043/F, dt. 15.4.1976);
   extended to regular employees of aided non-Government educational
   institutions, State Government universities/institutes, Urban Local Bodies,
   and Odisha Group-B/C/D contractual appointees; and — the single most
   directly useful confirmation for this file's applicant-facing purpose —
   Rule/Clause text stating IN TERMS that "In case of retirement / relinquishing
   of service, the G.I.S Premium deposit (without interest) is to be refunded
   by the Concerned Head of Offices." The CURRENT premium/cover SLABS
   themselves (this Resolution is a procedural/administrative circular that
   references, but does not itself restate, the substantive Resolution No.
   28557/F, dated 8.10.2021, which actually fixed them) were NOT independently
   read this session — the figures below (Grade Pay up to Rs. 4,800: premium
   Rs. 20,000/year, cover Rs. 4 lakh; Grade Pay Rs. 5,400 and above: premium
   Rs. 30,000/year, cover Rs. 6 lakh; last-rites assistance Rs. 10,000; premium
   deducted in 10 salary installments) rest on a single secondary news source
   (Sambad English) reporting that same 2021 revision, not the Resolution's own
   text — confirm your own current slab and cover amount with your DDO/HRMS
   before relying on either figure.

   FAMILY PENSION & DCRG — via caodisha.nic.in (the Controller of Accounts,
   Odisha's own FAQ page, a government-domain administrative source), fetched
   this session: Family Pension at the Normal Rate is a minimum of 30% of last
   drawn emoluments; at the Enhanced Rate, "equal to the pension the deceased
   employee was entitled to," payable for 10 years (NOTE: this "10 years" flat
   duration, as directly stated by this source, is presented as-is — it was
   NOT cross-checked against the Odisha Civil Services (Pension) Rules, 1992's
   own text this session, and differs in its framing from the more familiar
   "7 years or until age 67" formula several peer states in this app use, so it
   may reflect either a genuine Odisha-specific rule or a simplification on the
   FAQ page). The Death-cum-Retirement Gratuity ceiling has been revised
   several times per the same page: Rs. 2.5 lakh up to 31.12.2005, Rs. 7.5 lakh
   from 1.1.2006, Rs. 15 lakh from 1.1.2016, and Rs. 20 lakh from 1.1.2024 (Rs.
   7.5 lakh from 1.1.2024 specifically for aided-educational-institution
   employees) — this is a genuinely more recent revision than several peer
   states' files in this app have found for their own DCRG ceilings. Governing
   instrument: the Odisha Civil Services (Pension) Rules, 1992 (this session
   located, but did not read cover-to-cover, the Rules' own hosted text at
   finance.odisha.gov.in).

   ADVANCES — MIXED PRIMARY/SECONDARY, ONE FULLY PRIMARY-VERBATIM FIGURE: this
   session confirmed via the Odisha General Financial Rules, 2023 (fetched and
   read via pdftotext, current to 4.6.2025) that OGFR ITSELF DOES NOT STATE
   HBA/Motor Car/Computer Advance terms — its own Rule 309(ii) states in terms
   "Advances sanctioned in favour of Government employees shall be governed by
   separate provision notified by Finance Department and as amended from time
   to time," i.e. these live in standalone Finance Department Office
   Memoranda, not OGFR's own text (an important correction against an
   AI-search-summary this session initially received, which wrongly attributed
   HBA/MCA/Computer Advance to specific OGFR rule numbers that this session's
   own reading of the current OGFR text shows govern UNRELATED procurement
   matters instead — that summary's rule numbers are NOT used anywhere below).
   FESTIVAL ADVANCE IS FULLY PRIMARY-VERBATIM: this session fetched and read
   Finance Department O.M. No. 32216/F, dated 6.10.2018 (FIN-CS2-ADV-0012-2018)
   in full — enhanced from Rs. 15,000 to Rs. 20,000, recoverable in a maximum
   of 10 equal monthly instalments of Rs. 2,000 each, restricted to employees
   in Pay Level 9 and below (ORSP Rules, 2017 State Pay Matrix), with
   non-recovery within the ten months barring the next Festival Advance until
   the earlier one is cleared. HOUSE BUILDING ADVANCE figures (50 months' basic
   pay subject to a maximum of Rs. 40 lakh for purchase/construction, or 50
   months' pay/Rs. 10 lakh for enlargement/completion; 8% p.a. simple interest;
   repayment within 25 years with an 18-month moratorium; 5 years' minimum
   service) come from a single secondary source (a practitioner blog,
   worksodisha.blogspot.com, styling itself as tracking Odisha Secretariat
   Service rules) citing Finance Department O.M. No. 17502/F, dated 13.7.2022 —
   THAT SAME SOURCE, AND THE FINANCE DEPARTMENT'S OWN NOTIFICATION-INDEX PAGE,
   BOTH FLAG A FURTHER REVISION (Notification No. 32622, dated around
   26.11-26.12.2024) WHOSE UPDATED FIGURES THIS SESSION COULD NOT
   INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRM — the 2022 figures above may therefore be superseded;
   confirm the current ceiling with your DDO/the Finance Department before
   applying. MOTOR CAR / MOTOR CYCLE ADVANCE (interest-bearing, conventional):
   confirmed to exist (Motor Car: max 100 monthly instalments, interest over a
   further max 25 instalments, per FDOM No. 16153, dt. 3.6.2015 for minimum
   eligibility; Motor Cycle/Scooter: max 60 instalments, interest over max 10)
   from the same secondary blog source — NO rupee ceiling for either was
   independently confirmed this session; do not assume a specific amount.
   ELECTRIC VEHICLE ADVANCE — INTEREST-FREE, TIME-LIMITED, FLAGGED AS
   POSSIBLY EXPIRED: DIRECTLY READ (via secondary reporting of Finance
   Department O.M. No. 8524/F, dated 5.4.2022, FIN-CS2-ADV-0001-2022) — an
   INTEREST-FREE advance for an electric motor car, Group-A/B employees only,
   capped at Rs. 15 lakh or 75% of the ex-showroom price (whichever is lower),
   recoverable in 100 equal monthly instalments; a parallel electric two-
   wheeler advance for Group-C/D and above, capped at Rs. 2 lakh (also 75%/
   ex-showroom, whichever lower). THIS SESSION'S OWN SOURCE STATES THE SCHEME'S
   APPLICATION WINDOW WAS VALID ONLY THROUGH 31 DECEMBER 2025, LATER EXTENDED
   TO 28 FEBRUARY 2026 — which, as of this file being written (server date
   30 July 2026), APPEARS TO HAVE ALREADY LAPSED. This type is included anyway
   (the underlying policy may well have been renewed/extended again, which
   this session's searches did not surface either way), but with an explicit,
   prominent flag that the window may be closed — confirm current
   availability with your DDO/the Finance Department before relying on it.
   COMPUTER ADVANCE — confirmed to exist and to have been revised via O.M.
   No. 22542/F, dated 3.7.2013 ("both the ceiling of advance and monetary
   limit revised"), but this session could not locate the current rupee
   ceiling itself — use the advance type to request current terms rather than
   assuming a figure.

   DELIBERATELY OMITTED, WITH REASONS: Hospital Leave, Special Disability
   Leave, Study Leave (named in passing in the 1966 Rules' own definitions
   section as a leave category but with no rule text located this session
   describing its terms), Miscarriage/Abortion Leave and Adoption Leave were
   not independently confirmed with day-counts or current rule numbers this
   session — omitted rather than assumed, following this app's standing
   conservative pattern. A distinct Odisha indemnity-bond form number for
   deceased employees' dues (analogous to Kerala's K.F.C. Form No. 9) was not
   confirmed — the Indemnity Bond type below is accordingly generic, matching
   the pattern already used for Jharkhand in this app.
   ============================================================================= */
